\begin_layout Title
Introduction and History of Experimental Economics
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Date
August 29, 2013
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The First Welfare Theorem, which is often associated with Adam Smith's Invisible
 Hand Theorem, is one of the most important results in economics.
 With relatively few assumptions, it claims that any competitive equilibrium
 allocation is Pareto efficient.
 Moreover, the existence of a competitive equilibrium for general production
 economics was proved in Debreu (1959).
 However, there is debate between economists over the conditions necessary
 for the actual attainment of a competitive equilibrium.
 The primary assumptions for perfectly competitive markets are: (1) every
 participant is a price-taker; (2) there is perfect information.
 While many economists believe these conjectures are imperative for a competitiv
e equlibrium, there are still others who argue that the propositions still
 hold even when the assumptions are relaxed.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The three assigned papers all dealt with experiments on competitive market
 behavior.
 The authors were interested in analyzing the predictive power of the theories
 within the labratory environment.
 So, the experiments developed served to check the robustness of the competitive
 market model.
 Simple labratory markets were created, each with its own unique set of
 trading rules.
 In most of the experiments, private single unit values and costs were randomly
 assigned to dozens of participants, who acted as buyers and sellers over
 a single commodity.
 The primary goal of the experiments was to test and measure the effect
 of different forms of market organization on the efficiency, performance,
 convergance pattern, and equilibrium level of prices (Plott and Smith,
 1978).
 In addition, the authors hoped to discover empirical regularities in order
 to provide a foundation for more formal development of theory.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Chamberlin (1948) allowed time for buyers and sellers to interact with one
 another in order to search for a partner, so they could reach a contract
 over the single commodity.
 This pure exchange market was only operated for a single trading period.
 The experiment was conducted a total of forty-six times, and the average
 price was higher than the equilibrium price seven times and lower thirty-nine
 times.
 Even though the artificial market contained 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

imperfections,
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 which are often overlooked in the theoretical model, the results still
 lead Chamberlin to question the reasoning that prices converge to the equilibri
um in a competitive market.
 On the other hand, when smaller submarkets were analyzed, the results coincided
 with the predictions of the model.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Then, Smith (1962) presented a different version of the experiment conducted
 by Chamberlin.
 Smith used a double-auction mechanism, instead of bilateral search, in
 which buyers and sellers make public bids.
 The trading took place over several periods while the value and cost assignment
s were usually held constant.
 About different 10 tests were run, each a unique variation of the original
 setup.
 It was discovered that in almost all cases, the average actual price quickly
 converged to the equilibrium value.
 Finally, Smith was able to construct an empirical model of the data, in
 which he formulated the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

excess-rent hypothesis.
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 The assertion is that prices in competitive markets tend to rise and fall
 in proportion to the total excess rent corresponding to any transaction
 price.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Finally, Plott and Smith (1978) examined two separate experiments with the
 goal of rebuffing certain claims made in Williams (1973).
 In his experiment, Williams ran a discriminative posted-price institution
 in order to analyze the one-sided studies presented in Smith (1962), who
 ran a compeititve oral-price auction.
 Evidently, Williams obtained results that were the exact opposite of those
 reported by Smith.
 However, Plott and Smith argued that the reason for the discrepancy lies
 in the distinct theoretical predictions between a discriminative and competitiv
e sealed-bid auction.
 After running the two separate experiments, Plott and Smith were able to
 verify the hypothesized differences in experimental results.
\end_layout

